> is it still intended that [Object:null] is "different" than null?
`null` is a variant of type NIL
`[Object:null]` is a variant of type OBJECT with a null pointer.

To be honest this has pros and cons. It can be a little weird and unexpected to have a behaviour like that, but on the other hand it helps debugging issues. So I don't know if that behaviour should be changed. If this behaviour is to be changed, I guess it would be to make the Variant constructors to return a NIL variant if a null pointer is given.

```cpp
// variant_construct.h:190, in VariantConstructorNilObject
	static void construct(Variant &r_ret, const Variant **p_args, Callable::CallError &r_error) {
		if (p_args[0]->get_type() != Variant::NIL) {
			r_error.error = Callable::CallError::CALL_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT;
			r_error.argument = 0;
			r_error.expected = Variant::NIL;
		}

		VariantInternal::clear(&r_ret);
		VariantInternal::object_assign_null(&r_ret);
	}
```

Considering this constructor, I would say this behaviour is indeed the intended one.

comments:
After investigation, the argument passed to the setter is indeed of type `Variant::OBJECT` with a null pointer. 
I guess the issue is that since the pointer is null, casting it to the requested type (here `Node`) fails.

The issue boils down to `GDScriptDataType::is_type`. In this particular case, this function tries to retreive the object corresponding to the null object through the `ObjectDB` but since it's null a null pointer is returned, which makes `is_type` returns falls : 
```cpp
// gdscript_function.h:108
				Object *obj = p_variant.get_validated_object();
				if (!obj) {
					return false;
				}
```

In my opinion, in this case `is_type` should return true since all `Object`-derived classes can be null.
